// The walking skeleton: five rows of CSV in, five `Person` vertices out, in
// Apache GraphAr layout. Run it from the repository root —
//
//     cargo run --bin fossil -- run examples/hello.fossil --dest file:///tmp/hello
//
// — and `/tmp/hello/vertex/Person/*.parquet` holds the rows,
// `/tmp/hello/vertex/Person.vertex.yml` declares their columns. `README.md` has
// the DuckDB one-liner that reads them back.
//
// This is the one program asserted by CONTENT rather than by exit code:
// `crates/fossil-cli/tests/walking_skeleton.rs` checks the five subjects
// `https://example.org/user/1`…`/5` and the five names on a `name` column. It
// may be made clearer; it may not be made to mean something else.
//
// Every path it names is resolved against THIS FILE, never against the
// directory you ran `fossil` from — `users.csv` and `hello.shex` sit beside it.

// The output contract, and it is MANDATORY (ruling 3, 2026-08-11). A property
// key is the last segment of a predicate IRI some shape declares, so with no
// shape document in scope the `name` below resolves against nothing, the mapping
// writes no properties — and the run still SUCCEEDS, five vertices with the
// column simply absent. `hello.shex` declares `ex:name xsd:string` at 1..1,
// which is what makes `name` a key that exists and a key that must be written.
type { Person } := io.shex("hello.shex")

// The source. Its columns are introspected from the CSV, not declared here, and
// `User` is the name every reference in the body is qualified by.
User := io.csv("users.csv")

// The header reads: a mapping called `People`, of shape `Person`, over the rows
// of `User`. The vertex type on disk is `Person` — the local name of the shape's
// IRI, never the mapping's own name.
People : Person from User
    // The identity, and every mapping needs exactly one: it is what turns a row
    // into a node. An ordinary quoted string whose `{…}` holes are ordinary
    // expressions, evaluated per row.
    @subject = "https://example.org/user/{User.id}"
    // A property. Bare key on the left, because the shape document is what says
    // which keys exist; qualified reference on the right, because the source
    // binding is what says where the value comes from.
    name     = User.name
